WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.730
Mind Your Decisions, I'm Presh Talwalkar.

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:12.120
Inside a semicircle, two side-by-side squares are inscribed. If the semicircle's radius is equal to 10,

00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:16.260
can you solve for the total area of the two squares?

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:17.949
If no,

00:00:17.949 --> 00:00:20.429
demonstrate why not. If yes,

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:22.960
calculate the answer.

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:25.710
This problem is adapted from Math StackExchange.

00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:33.689
Be sure to consider all possible ways that two side by side squares could be inscribed in the semicircle.

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:41.459
Pause if you'd like to give this problem a try, and when you're ready, keep watching to learn how to solve this problem.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:58.320
In a right triangle with legs of a and b and a hypotenuse c,

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:04.080
we all know that a^2 + b^2 = c^2. But what should this rule be called?

00:01:04.989 --> 00:01:12.989
According to mathematician Manjul Bhargava, who's a Fields Medal winner,the Pythagorean theorem is clearly the wrong name.

00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:16.229
It should either be an Egyptian theorem,

00:01:16.229 --> 00:01:20.909
if you look at the standard of just having an idea about it; an Indian theorem,

00:01:20.909 --> 00:01:26.699
if you're looking for a complete statement of it; or a Chinese theorem, if you're looking for the proof of it.

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:33.300
I'm preferential towards the proof of it, and I suggest we call it the Gougu theorem,

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:36.119
which is what the theorem is known as in China.

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:41.729
Getting the history correct is very important, as Manjul Bhargava explains:

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:45.750
"Knowing the correct history of mathematics was useful in my own research,

00:01:46.179 --> 00:01:51.029
because if you learned from the original source how an idea came about, that can give you great insight."

00:01:52.479 --> 00:01:59.309
Now, let's solve the problem: suppose one square has a side length of x, and the other square has a side length of y.

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:07.079
The total area of the two squares will be equal to x^2 + y^2. Draw the diagonal of the x square.

00:02:07.509 --> 00:02:14.369
Its diagonal length will be equal to x times the square root of 2, and the diagonal will bisect the right angle into two

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:16.530
45 degree angles.

00:02:16.660 --> 00:02:18.660
Now draw the other diagonal.

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:24.779
Its length will be equal to y times the square root of 2, and it will form a 45-degree angle right here.

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:29.250
Thus, the two diagonals form a 90 degree right angle.

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:33.899
Draw a cord connecting these diagonals, and call it z.

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:40.440
This is a right triangle, so we can use the Gougu theorem to deduce the length of z squared.

00:02:40.959 --> 00:02:44.189
This simplifies to be 2x squared + 2y squared.

00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:47.910
We'll now figure out the measure of this circular arc.

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:55.199
We'll focus on this diagram,and reflect the entire diagram across the diameter.

00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:59.219
What is the measure of this inscribed angle?

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:04.649
It will bisect the right angle of the square. Therefore, it'll be equal to 45 degrees

00:03:05.290 --> 00:03:10.209
We can thus calculate the measure of this circular arc by the inscribed angle theorem.

00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:13.449
It'll be equal to two times 45 degrees or 90 degrees.

00:03:14.689 --> 00:03:21.339
How does this help us? We'll go back to our diagram and consider another way to calculate this chord z.

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:27.129
From the center of the circle, draw radii to the endpoints of the chord.

00:03:27.769 --> 00:03:35.709
Since this circular arc is 90 degrees, this central angle will be equal to 90 degrees as well; it'll be a right angle.

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:41.530
We can thus apply the Gougu theorem again to get z squared is equal to r^2+  r^2.

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:44.829
We now have two equations which will simplify:

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:48.909
the second equation simplifies to be 2r^2, and

00:03:49.190 --> 00:03:52.839
since both equations are equal to C squared, we set them equal to each other.

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:57.429
We then divide by 2 to get x^ 2 + y^2 = r^2.

00:03:58.190 --> 00:04:02.649
Thus the total area will be equal to r^2 if r = 10.

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:06.849
It's equal to 10^2, which is equal to 100 and that's our answer.

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:10.629
Regardless of how the two side by side squares are inscribed,

00:04:11.510 --> 00:04:13.280
Incredible!

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:17.559
Thanks for making mind your decisions one of the best channels on YouTube, and as

00:04:18.289 --> 00:04:21.999
always thanks for watching and thanks for your support.

